1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Four winter festivals bring ice fishing

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:05,320
snow sculptures

3
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:08,080
Seoul lanterns and grilled trout.

4
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:09,760
Here are the best picks

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:12,020
how to reach them and what to check before

6
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:12,760
you go.

7
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:15,966
Here are the festival locations

8
00:00:15,966 --> 00:00:19,526
from Gangwon’s frozen rivers to Seoul’s city lights.

9
00:00:19,884 --> 00:00:22,824
Start with the famous ice festival

10
00:00:22,824 --> 00:00:25,364
then choose another event if snow

11
00:00:25,364 --> 00:00:28,904
skiing or a quieter local feel matters more to you.

12
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:33,250
These are alternatives spread across the province

13
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:35,510
not stops on one easy day route.

14
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:38,980
Hwacheon Sancheoneo Ice Festival is the

15
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:40,224
best-known event.

16
00:00:40,224 --> 00:00:41,778
On the frozen river

17
00:00:41,778 --> 00:00:45,447
visitors fish through small holes with short rods.

18
00:00:45,447 --> 00:00:48,369
There are also ice sculptures, sledging

19
00:00:48,369 --> 00:00:49,675
ice football and

20
00:00:49,675 --> 00:00:50,794
for the brave

21
00:00:50,794 --> 00:00:52,400
bare-hand fishing.

22
00:00:52,818 --> 00:00:56,421
Hwacheon usually runs for about three weeks in January.

23
00:00:56,421 --> 00:00:59,193
The twenty twenty-seven festival is planned

24
00:00:59,193 --> 00:01:01,410
for January ninth to thirty-first

25
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:03,406
but thin ice can change that.

26
00:01:03,406 --> 00:01:07,198
Check the official site a few days before travelling.

27
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:11,140
The riverbank stalls can grill or prepare the

28
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:12,500
fish you catch

29
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:14,500
and there is usually a fishing area for

30
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,220
foreign visitors.

31
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:17,280
From Seoul

32
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:19,920
take an intercity bus or book a day tour.

33
00:01:20,286 --> 00:01:23,986
Pyeongchang Trout Festival takes place in Jinbu

34
00:01:23,986 --> 00:01:26,946
usually from late December into January.

35
00:01:26,946 --> 00:01:28,706
Try ice fishing, sledging

36
00:01:28,706 --> 00:01:30,846
snowmobiles and ice slides.

37
00:01:30,846 --> 00:01:34,066
Jinbu is reachable by KTX on the Gangneung line

38
00:01:34,066 --> 00:01:36,446
and skiing nearby makes a good pairing.

39
00:01:36,888 --> 00:01:41,608
Choose Taebaek if you want snow more than fishing.

40
00:01:41,608 --> 00:01:44,768
Its festival is known for large snow sculptures

41
00:01:44,768 --> 00:01:47,928
snow slides and a winter climb on Taebaeksan.

42
00:01:47,928 --> 00:01:52,048
It usually falls from late January into early February.

43
00:01:52,458 --> 00:01:54,492
Inje is smaller and more local

44
00:01:54,492 --> 00:01:57,489
with fishing for tiny bingeo on a frozen lake.

45
00:01:57,489 --> 00:02:00,004
It was not held from twenty twenty-four

46
00:02:00,004 --> 00:02:02,895
through twenty twenty-six because the lake did

47
00:02:02,895 --> 00:02:04,072
not freeze safely

48
00:02:04,072 --> 00:02:06,962
so confirm that it is running before planning

49
00:02:06,962 --> 00:02:07,958
around it.

50
00:02:08,268 --> 00:02:10,888
For a simpler evening

51
00:02:10,888 --> 00:02:11,468
stay in the city.

52
00:02:11,468 --> 00:02:14,488
Or trade the streets for a garden day trip

53
00:02:14,488 --> 00:02:16,168
filled with winter lights.

54
00:02:16,638 --> 00:02:19,278
Seoul’s lights are central;

55
00:02:19,278 --> 00:02:22,078
the Garden of Morning Calm is a separate trip

56
00:02:22,078 --> 00:02:23,338
outside the city.

57
00:02:24,288 --> 00:02:27,248
The Seoul Lantern Festival lines

58
00:02:27,248 --> 00:02:29,588
Cheonggyecheon stream with illuminated

59
00:02:29,588 --> 00:02:31,508
lanterns and art.

60
00:02:31,508 --> 00:02:33,648
It is free and easy to reach on foot

61
00:02:33,648 --> 00:02:36,988
from Gwanghwamun or Jonggak stations.

62
00:02:36,988 --> 00:02:40,288
Go on a weekday evening to avoid the biggest crowds.

63
00:02:40,938 --> 00:02:44,378
The Seoul Winter Festa has also included a

64
00:02:44,378 --> 00:02:47,338
Christmas market in Gwanghwamun Square and an

65
00:02:47,338 --> 00:02:49,318
ice rink at Seoul Plaza.

66
00:02:49,318 --> 00:02:51,218
Dates change each year and are usually

67
00:02:51,218 --> 00:02:52,678
announced in late autumn

68
00:02:52,678 --> 00:02:54,078
so check before you go.

69
00:02:54,708 --> 00:02:56,788
For a bigger light display

70
00:02:56,788 --> 00:02:58,988
the Garden of Morning Calm in Gapyeong

71
00:02:58,988 --> 00:03:01,428
decorates its paths

72
00:03:01,428 --> 00:03:02,968
trees and hills.

73
00:03:02,968 --> 00:03:05,348
It is often combined with Nami Island on a

74
00:03:05,348 --> 00:03:06,908
day trip.

75
00:03:06,908 --> 00:03:09,968
Boseong’s green tea fields also light up in winter.

76
00:03:10,422 --> 00:03:11,381
For Hwacheon

77
00:03:11,381 --> 00:03:14,616
buses from Dong Seoul take roughly two and a

78
00:03:14,616 --> 00:03:16,773
half to three hours each way.

79
00:03:16,773 --> 00:03:17,672
Leave early;

80
00:03:17,672 --> 00:03:20,968
an early bus gives you more time on the ice.

81
00:03:20,968 --> 00:03:23,245
Day tours are an easier option

82
00:03:23,245 --> 00:03:25,642
especially on a busy weekend.

83
00:03:27,572 --> 00:03:30,274
Pyeongchang’s Jinbu station is on the KTX

84
00:03:30,274 --> 00:03:31,266
Gangneung line.

85
00:03:31,266 --> 00:03:34,078
Taebaek is about three and a half to four

86
00:03:34,078 --> 00:03:36,338
hours from Seoul by bus or train.

87
00:03:36,338 --> 00:03:40,332
Check current routes and times on Naver Map or KakaoMap.

88
00:03:40,842 --> 00:03:43,102
Dress for standing still

89
00:03:43,102 --> 00:03:44,782
not just walking.

90
00:03:44,782 --> 00:03:46,102
Wear thermal layers

91
00:03:46,102 --> 00:03:47,362
a padded coat

92
00:03:47,362 --> 00:03:48,422
waterproof boots

93
00:03:48,422 --> 00:03:50,662
a hat and gloves.

94
00:03:50,662 --> 00:03:53,242
Sitting on ice gets cold quickly.

95
00:03:53,652 --> 00:03:57,652
Heat packs are easy to find at convenience stores.

96
00:03:57,652 --> 00:03:59,672
Bring some cash for food stalls

97
00:03:59,672 --> 00:04:02,232
although cards are widely accepted.

98
00:04:02,232 --> 00:04:04,472
Check festival details and what to wear before

99
00:04:04,472 --> 00:04:05,352
you set out.

100
00:04:05,622 --> 00:04:06,545
If you can

101
00:04:06,545 --> 00:04:07,837
choose a weekday.

102
00:04:07,837 --> 00:04:10,730
The first weekend is especially crowded

103
00:04:10,730 --> 00:04:12,699
fishing areas can fill up

104
00:04:12,699 --> 00:04:16,022
and fish tend to bite better in the morning.

105
00:04:16,022 --> 00:04:17,007
Book tickets

106
00:04:17,007 --> 00:04:20,322
tours and busy-weekend return buses ahead.

107
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:23,540
Three avoidable mistakes:

108
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:25,600
assuming the ice is safe

109
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:26,700
arriving late

110
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:29,300
and treating the Gangwon festivals as one

111
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:30,180
short trip.

112
00:04:30,498 --> 00:04:32,895
Ice festivals depend on safe ice;

113
00:04:32,895 --> 00:04:34,572
mild weather can delay

114
00:04:34,572 --> 00:04:36,250
shorten or cancel them.

115
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:39,306
Arrive early because fishing areas fill up.

116
00:04:39,306 --> 00:04:41,044
And check journey times:

117
00:04:41,044 --> 00:04:43,620
Hwacheon alone takes hours each way

118
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:45,418
so plan a full day.

119
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:47,754
When is the Hwacheon festival?

120
00:04:47,754 --> 00:04:50,689
It is planned for January ninth to thirty-first

121
00:04:50,689 --> 00:04:51,874
twenty twenty-seven

122
00:04:51,874 --> 00:04:53,367
at the time of writing.

123
00:04:53,367 --> 00:04:55,736
It usually runs for about three weeks

124
00:04:55,736 --> 00:04:59,280
but check the official site because dates can change.

125
00:04:59,814 --> 00:05:03,554
Can you visit Hwacheon from Seoul in one day?

126
00:05:03,554 --> 00:05:04,834
Yes.

127
00:05:04,834 --> 00:05:06,794
Buses take about two and a half to three

128
00:05:06,794 --> 00:05:08,094
hours each way

129
00:05:08,094 --> 00:05:09,714
so leave early.

130
00:05:09,714 --> 00:05:11,614
A tour is another option

131
00:05:11,614 --> 00:05:13,554
especially on a busy weekend.

132
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:17,600
What if the ice is too thin?

133
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:19,460
Fishing can be delayed

134
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:21,500
reduced or cancelled.

135
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:24,880
Organisers post changes on their official sites.

136
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:26,360
Check a few days ahead

137
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:28,700
then check again on the morning you travel.

138
00:05:29,082 --> 00:05:32,302
Choose Hwacheon for ice fishing

139
00:05:32,302 --> 00:05:34,342
Pyeongchang for trout and skiing

140
00:05:34,342 --> 00:05:35,522
Taebaek for snow

141
00:05:35,522 --> 00:05:37,802
or Inje for a smaller event.

142
00:05:37,802 --> 00:05:38,962
For easy lights

143
00:05:38,962 --> 00:05:40,262
stay in Seoul;

144
00:05:40,262 --> 00:05:42,582
check every festival’s dates and ice conditions.

145
00:05:43,092 --> 00:05:44,175
The full guide

146
00:05:44,175 --> 00:05:46,627
with maps and links for every stop

147
00:05:46,627 --> 00:05:48,965
is free at Korea Plainly dot com.

148
00:05:48,965 --> 00:05:51,930
The link is at the top of the description.

149
00:05:51,930 --> 00:05:53,014
If this helped

150
00:05:53,014 --> 00:05:54,724
subscribe for more plain

151
00:05:54,724 --> 00:05:56,792
practical guides to Korea.

